WEBVTT

c01
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.450
Remember the two cases?

c02
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.510
They come from different decades, but they teach the same lesson.

c03
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.230
Map the process first, and only then change it.

c04
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.830
Now, let's apply it.

c05
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:20.820
This is the HR case from Lesson 1, a services company with about 400 employees.

c06
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:26.330
The HR director wants a chatbot on the HR inbox, and wants it live next month.

c07
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.040
Three roles matter here.

c08
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:31.840
There is the HR director, who asked for the chatbot.

c09
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:35.460
There are the HR staff, who answer employees every day.

c10
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.580
And there is the HR manager, who owns the policies.

c11
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.120
HR gets about 90 requests a month, and most of them are repeats,

c12
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.370
about vacation days, pension, expenses and overtime.

c13
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.770
They arrive by WhatsApp, by email, and in person.

c14
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.830
There's no system, and nobody knows how many of them actually need human judgment.

c15
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.730
The tempting first move is a chatbot, but not yet.

c16
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:07.310
First, we map the process as it is, the way it actually happens on a normal day,

c17
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.920
with no fixing and no AI.

c18
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.070
Field one is the process name, which should be plain and specific.

c19
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.630
Here, it is answering employees' repeat questions to HR.

c20
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.650
Field two is the trigger, the concrete thing that starts the process.

c21
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.330
It could be a message, a form, or a date.

c22
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.910
Before I fill it in, you choose.

c23
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.080
All three channels go on the map, because all three really happen.

c24
00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.640
Where a process starts and where it ends is a decision.

c25
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.100
Without clear boundaries, it swells until it covers the whole organization.

c26
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.550
Field three is the steps, each one with a role owner.

c27
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.930
Here, there are three steps, receiving the request,

c28
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.050
checking the topic, and then an answer or an escalation.

c29
00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.560
Owners are roles, not names.

c30
00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.200
That way, the map stays true, even when people change.

c31
00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.730
Each role gets its own lane, so you can always see who is holding the work.

c32
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:09.540
Field four asks where responsibility passes from one role to another.

c33
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.200
Here, it passes from the HR staff to the HR manager,

c34
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.390
when a question goes beyond known policy.

c35
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:24.200
That's the most dangerous moment in a process, because the staffer assumes the manager knows what

c36
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.190
happened before.

c37
00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.680
A good map marks each handoff.

c38
00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:33.560
It shows from whom, to whom, what passes, and what happens if the receiver is unavailable.

c39
00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.110
Here, there's no single channel for it.

c40
00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.360
Field five is knowledge sources.

c41
00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:45.900
Here, they are the vacation, pension, expense, and overtime policies.

c42
00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.740
Each one gets an owner, which here is the HR manager,

c43
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.760
and a sensitivity tag.

c44
00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.960
The tag can be public, internal, confidential, or restricted.

c45
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:03.230
These policies are internal, but an employee's personal or financial details are confidential,

c46
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.670
so they never go into a public AI tool.

c47
00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.540
Field six is existing controls.

c48
00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.540
Is anything checking the answers before they go out?

c49
00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.850
So, what goes in the map?

c50
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.610
There is no formal control.

c51
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.190
It depends on the staffer's own judgment.

c52
00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:26.070
And that's a valid answer, because it's exactly what mapping reveals.

c53
00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:30.940
Field seven is gaps, and here you can simply write that it is unclear.

c54
00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:36.540
Nobody knows how many wrong answers were given, and there's no single handoff channel.

c55
00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.970
That's a diagnostic result.

c56
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.770
Field eight is the success metric.

c57
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:47.230
Today, about 35% of the HR team's time goes to repeat questions.

c58
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.650
The target is 20%, within three months.

c59
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.850
That is a number you can check later.

c60
00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.820
Next, you fill in field five yourself.
